T-749-Detail [eb424186a6ce] <<LAND223>> The ticket's fix shape (one cell per mutant in the RD-684 re-drive suite, each red under its mutant and green on main), read against the independent review. M-B4 (only ENOENT resolves): the cell F7 turned red under each of three plants and no other cell did. M-B5 (the dedupe): the cell F8 turned red. M-B6 (the early read): the cell F9 turned red. M-B7 (the field carried by the run's own push): the fourth new cell turned red. By the builder's record every one of these mutants survived at the base. What the cells assert: F7, a lookup error other than not found (a path through a regular file, a self-pointing link) is not proof the data is gone, so both carried failures stay; F8, a run that has its own verdict on a file is not doubled by the earlier ledger naming the same file; F9, the earlier ledger is read before the run starts, so a hook that empties it mid-run does not lose the failure; the fourth, the failure entry of the run itself carries its own survivingTarget field, checked in the stored ledger and in the failure detail. The review read the fourth cell against the code: the run's own push goes into the one failed array that is both written to the ledger and returned, so the stored form is the one the next run reads; the cell would not catch a mutant that strips the field only from the returned object, which is not M-B7, and the review kept it as written. The cell for M-B4 asserts both error codes first (a path through a regular file and a self-pointing link), so a platform difference fails loudly and never silently green; the review read the four cells for platform dependence and found none (it did not run them on Linux or Node 24). The file went from 7 to 11 cells and no suite was added. Findings of the review on this pull request, all Minor and none blocking: a wording slip in the build record (11 old titles named for this file, which held 7); a counts-file conflict with main, resolved at landing by regenerating the counts; M-A1 and M-A3 of the sister ticket equivalent through HTTP; that the merge starts the demo deploy workflow, whose jobs read skipped on this merge; and Node 24 and Linux not run locally. Not measured: Linux lstat codes for the two error cases, and the hosted Build on Node 24 beyond what is quoted above. [LAND-223-749-18NOV]
