T-NEW-P3 [047f3723893c] Finding 1 (Minor): one fail-open shape remains, a report that is internally inconsistent. A version 2 report whose vulnerabilities entry is an empty object or an empty list while its metadata says high 5 passes the audit (exit status 0, with a note only); before the change, npm's own exit status of 1 would have failed it. The script checks the shape of the vulnerabilities entry and traces every blocking node to an advisory, but never cross-checks the metadata counts. npm 11 does not emit such a report (the real report has 5 blocking nodes and metadata high 5), and every other bad shape tried by the review failed closed. Proposed: fail when the metadata total of moderate, high and critical advisories is not equal to the number of blocking entries in vulnerabilities, or when the metadata is absent; add one cell.
T-NEW-P4 [d4bc095e58d9] Finding 2 (Minor): the words 'or on a fix' are honoured only through the range text. The entry stops matching if npm reports another range or another package (the review planted changed range texts, a smaller bound, a spaced form and a combined range, and another package: all failed). If picomatch 4.0.8 ships and the advisory keeps the range <=4.0.7, which would still be accurate, the entry keeps allowing it until 2026-12-18. The comment at the top of scripts/audit-prod.js and the commit message state as fact that a patched release changes the range text; that was not measured. The npm fixAvailable flag cannot be used naively: it already reads truthy on all 5 nodes today (npm proposes a semver-major move of sqlite3), so a check on it would lapse the exception now. Measured by the review: advisory high, range <= 4.0.7, no first patched version, not withdrawn, and 4.0.7 the newest picomatch on npm. The remedy is the calendar check above; a lapse on a newer published version is not available offline in CI.
